# Where the data lives: the DuckDB catalog, one database file per dataset, and the
# uploaded originals. Several gigabytes, written to continuously while the app runs.
#
# This must NOT be inside OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive or any other syncing folder.
# A sync client copying a live .duckdb file mid-write captures a half-finished page, and
# the database restored from it is corrupt - silently, until the next time it is opened.
#
# It must also not live under AppData\Local on Windows. That looks like the obvious
# choice, but AppData\Local is redirected per-application for packaged (MSIX) apps: a
# tool running inside such a package writes to its own private LocalCache while the rest
# of the machine reads the real folder and finds nothing. Both paths are identical in
# every message the system prints, so the split stays invisible until something looks.
#
# Copy this file to .env and set your own path. A plain directory at the root of a drive
# is neither synced nor redirected, which is the whole requirement. Back it up
# deliberately - see the Backups panel in Settings.
DATA_DIR=C:\CSVStudioData

# Optional: how many verified backups to keep, and where to put them. Defaults to inside
# DATA_DIR/backups if unset - fine as a first line of defense, but point this at a
# different drive to survive the data disk itself failing.
# BACKUP_DIR=D:\CSVStudioBackups
# BACKUP_KEEP=3

# Optional: overrides the auto-generated bootstrap admin account (see server startup log).
# ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me

# ---- serving this somewhere other than your own machine ----
#
# The address the app is reached at. Set it and two things follow on their own: the
# origin is allowed through CORS, and - if it is https - the session cookie gains its
# Secure flag, so the browser will no longer send it over an unencrypted connection.
#
# That flag matters more than it looks. The cookie IS the session: whoever holds it is
# signed in without a password. Without Secure, a single http request puts it on the
# wire in clear text for anyone on the same network to copy. Leave PUBLIC_ORIGIN unset
# for local development - over http://localhost a Secure cookie is discarded by the
# browser and nobody can sign in at all, which is why this cannot simply default to on.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://autoline.example.com

# Only needed if TLS is terminated by a proxy in front of this process: the app sees
# plain http and cannot work out that the browser is on https, so tell it.
# COOKIE_SECURE=true

# Only if the app is served from a different site than this API. Browsers accept
# SameSite=None solely alongside Secure, so setting this turns Secure on regardless of
# the two settings above.
# COOKIE_SAMESITE=none
